<sn><md>#### Finding `rclone.conf`

Run:

```bash
rclone config file
```

Example output:

```text
Configuration file is stored at:
/home/emon/.config/rclone/rclone.conf
```

On Linux, the usual location is:

```text
~/.config/rclone/rclone.conf
```
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Example Configuration

A Google Drive remote might look approximately like:

```ini
[gdrive]
type = drive
scope = drive
token = {"access_token":"...","token_type":"Bearer","refresh_token":"...","expiry":"..."}
team_drive =
```

The exact contents depend on your Rclone version and configuration.

**Understanding the Fields**

| Field            | Meaning                                   |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| `[gdrive]`       | Name of the Rclone remote                 |
| `type = drive`   | Google Drive backend                      |
| `scope = drive`  | Permission level granted to Rclone        |
| `token = ...`    | OAuth authentication information          |
| `team_drive =`   | Shared Drive ID, if using a Shared Drive  |
| `root_folder_id` | Optional folder to use as the remote root |
| `client_id`      | Optional custom Google API client ID      |
| `client_secret`  | Optional custom Google API client secret  |
</md></sn><sn><md>### Remote Name Comes From the Section

For:

```ini
[gdrive]
type = drive
```

the remote is:

```text
gdrive:
```

If you have:

```ini
[study]
type = drive
```

then the remote is:

```text
study:
```

So the relationship is:

```text
rclone.conf
     │
     └── [gdrive]
           │
           ▼
        gdrive:
           │
           ▼
      Google Drive
```
</md></sn><sn><md>### Important: `rclone.conf` Is Sensitive

The `token` can contain authentication credentials that allow Rclone to access your cloud storage.

&gt; [Do not share your complete ++rclone.conf++ publicly.]{.fg-red}

For example, avoid posting:

```ini
token = {"access_token":"REAL_TOKEN_HERE", ...}
```

If you need to show your configuration when troubleshooting, remove or replace the token first.

</md></sn><sn><md>### Managing the Configuration

You normally **shouldn't edit `rclone.conf` manually**. Use:

```bash
rclone config
```

For example:

```text
rclone config
   ↓
Create / Edit / Delete / Rename remote
   ↓
Rclone updates rclone.conf
```

To view the configuration path:

```bash
rclone config file
```

To see the configured remotes:

```bash
rclone listremotes
```

To display configuration:

```bash
rclone config show
```

For security, be careful with the last command because authentication information may be displayed.

</md></sn>